title ores.synthetic.service Component

set namespaceSeparator ::

namespace ores #F2F2F2 {
    namespace synthetic #F2F2F2 {
        namespace service #F2F2F2 {
            namespace app #F2F2F2 {
                class application {
                    +operator : application&
                }
                class application_exception {
                    -message_ : string
                }
                class host {
                }
            }
            namespace config #F2F2F2 {
                class options <<struct>> {
                    +logging : optional<ores::logging::logging_options>
                    +nats : ores::nats::config::nats_options
                    +database : ores::database::database_options
                }
                class parser {
                }
                class parser_exception {
                    -message_ : string
                }
            }
            namespace messaging #F2F2F2 {
                class event_registrar {
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

' The automated pass reads the public headers under service/include/, so the
' six types it draws are the process shell. Everything the service actually
' does -- the feed control plane, the event registrars and the feed controller
' -- lives in private headers under src/ that the pass never reads, and the
' shell's own boxes arrive empty because their content is methods.
'
' Redeclaring a class with its fully-qualified name merges the members into
' the box the automated pass already drew. No namespace block is reopened:
' this PlantUML version crashes when a namespace that already holds classes is
' declared a second time.

class ores::synthetic::service::app::application {
    +application()
    +run(io_ctx : io_context&, cfg : config::options) : awaitable<void>
    {static} -make_context(db_opts : database_options) : database::context
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::app::application"
The composition root, and the whole of the service's startup
order: open NATS, build the authenticated service-to-service
client, make the DB context, start the entity-change event
pipeline, ensure the two JetStream streams, construct the
feed_controller, run the boot-time auto-start walk, then hand
the handler-registration lambdas to ores.service's run()
before shutting the controller and the event source down.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::app::host {
    {static} +execute(args : vector<string>, std_output : ostream&, error_output : ostream&, io_ctx : io_context&) : awaitable<int>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::app::host"
The console workflow. The automated pass drew this box empty
because every member is a static method.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::config::parser {
    +parse(arguments : vector<string>, info : ostream&, error : ostream&) : optional<options>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::config::parser"
Returns an empty optional rather than throwing when the
command line asks for help or fails to parse, so the caller
decides the exit code. Logging is unavailable here by
design: the logger starts only after the parse succeeds, so
a parse error is reported on the error stream instead.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::messaging::event_registrar {
    {static} +register_event_mappings(event_source : postgres_event_source&, event_bus : event_bus&, nats : nats::client&) : vector<subscription>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::messaging::event_registrar"
The one hand-maintained line-per-entity call site in the
component. It composes the nine generated per-entity
registrars and takes ownership of the subscriptions they
return, so they outlive the call.
end note

' The nine generated per-entity event registrars. A free function cannot be
' drawn as a class, so each is redeclared here; together they are what the
' event_registrar above composes.

class ores::synthetic::service::messaging::register_folder_event_mapping {
    {static} +register_folder_event_mapping(event_source : postgres_event_source&, event_bus : event_bus&, nats : nats::client&) : subscription
}
note top of "ores::synthetic::service::messaging::register_folder_event_mapping"
Free function, one per entity, and the reason the box here
is hand-drawn: the automated pass reads types, not free
functions. Each registers the entity's Postgres NOTIFY
channel on the event source and subscribes the event bus,
so a committed row change becomes a NATS publish. The other
eight are named register_<entity>_event_mapping and carry
the same three arguments.
end note
note bottom of "ores::synthetic::service::messaging::register_folder_event_mapping"
The nine channels, one per synthetic table:
ores_synthetic_folders, _fx_spot_generation_configs,
_gmm_components, _ir_curve_generation_configs,
_ir_curve_generation_config_process_parameter_values,
_ir_curve_template_entries, _market_data_generation_configs,
_yield_curve_process_parameter_definitions and
_yield_curve_process_types. A row change on any of them
reaches NATS; no other synthetic table publishes an event.
end note

' The feed control plane: the controller, its handlers, and the private
' handlers the service registrars subscribe.

class ores::synthetic::service::feed_controller {
    +feed_controller(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&)
    +~feed_controller()
    +start(ore_key : string, source_name : string, means : vector<double>, stdevs : vector<double>, weights : vector<double>, initial_price : double, ticks_per_hour : double, process_type : string, vintage_source : string, vintage_date : string, error_detail : string*, caller_bearer_token : string, binding_mode : binding_mode, out_conflicting_source_name : string*) : start_result
    +start(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>) : start_result
    +add(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, binding_mode : binding_mode, out_conflicting_source_name : string*) : bool
    +add(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, out_conflicting_source_name : string*) : bool
    +stop(key : string) : size_t
    +shutdown()
    +running_count() : size_t
    +list(kind : string) : vector<string>
    +validate(ore_key : string, vintage_source : string, vintage_date : string, error_detail : string&, caller_bearer_token : string, resolved_price : double*) : bool
    +running_source_name_for_conflict_key(conflict_key : string) : optional<string>
    -ensure_feed_binding(ore_key : string, source_name : string, caller_bearer_token : string)
    -vintage_data_available(ore_key : string, vintage_source : string, vintage_date : string, error_detail : string&, caller_bearer_token : string, resolved_price : double*) : bool
    -find_conflict(feed : IFeed&, excluding_source_name : string) : optional<string>
    -start_running(feed : shared_ptr<IFeed>, binding_mode : binding_mode)
    -status_loop()
    -log_status()
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_controller"
One controller serves every asset class: feeds_ is keyed by
source_name, and every feed enters through the IFeed
interface, so the per-kind producers differ only in what
they publish. At most one feed runs per (qualifier, role)
pair -- feeds_conflict -- and a second one is refused by
name, never by stopping the first.
end note
note bottom of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_controller"
Three ways in, and the differences matter. add() is the
config-driven path (boot auto-start, the folder cascade):
the caller already built the feed, so no vintage check and
no binding creation happen here. start(IFeed) is the
on-demand path and distinguishes already_running from
qualifier_conflict. start(ore_key, ...) is the
client-supplied-params path: the only one that checks
vintage availability and auto-creates a feed_binding, and
it is FX-only. A sandboxed feed publishes on
synthetic.v1.sandbox.tick.<source> and never creates a
binding, so it is structurally unreachable from the
bound-feed ingest loop. One tick thread per feed, plus one
status thread that logs publish counts every minute.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::feed_config_handler {
    +feed_config_handler(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>)
    +start(msg : nats::message)
    +stop(msg : nats::message)
    +list(msg : nats::message)
    -start_fx(req_ctx : database::context&, msg : nats::message&, cfg : fx_spot_generation_config&)
    -start_ir(req_ctx : database::context&, msg : nats::message&, cfg : ir_curve_generation_config&)
    -reply_start_outcome(msg : nats::message&, resp : start_feed_response&, feed : shared_ptr<fx_spot_feed>)
    -authenticated_context(verb : string_view, msg : nats::message&) : optional<database::context>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::feed_config_handler"
Serves synthetic.v1.feed_configs.start / .stop / .list --
one handler for every asset class, replacing the per-kind
handlers that came before it. The client sends a config_id
and nothing else: the handler probes the FX repository and
then the IR repository to discover the kind, because the
market_data_generation_config container carries no kind
discriminator. Missing, disabled and vintage-missing all
answer with one message shape regardless of kind. start()
reads the container and the GMM components for FX, the
template entries and parameter values for IR, builds the
refdata context, then hands the feed to the factory and the
controller. stop() is idempotent: zero stopped means it was
already stopped.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::folder_feed_control_handler {
    +folder_feed_control_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>)
    +start(msg : nats::message)
    +stop(msg : nats::message)
    -resolve_subtree(ctx : database::context&, folder_id : uuid&) : set<uuid>
    -parse_folder_id(s : string&, out : uuid&) : bool
    -authenticated_context(verb : string_view, msg : nats::message&) : optional<database::context>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::folder_feed_control_handler"
Serves marketdata.v1.market_feed_configs.start_folder and
.stop_folder. The single place that turns "start everything
under this folder" into a sequence of producer starts: it
resolves the folder subtree once and fans out over every
config row beneath it, of every asset class, through the
same factory and controller -- so Qt, ores.shell and a wt
workflow step all get one behaviour from one request. The
folder and feed rows are tenant and party scoped under RLS,
so a valid JWT is required and the queries run in the
caller's own context. The parameter definitions catalogue is
read under the system tenant; everything else under the
caller's.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::vintage_validity_handler {
    +vintage_validity_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>)
    +list(msg : nats::message)
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::vintage_validity_handler"
Serves marketdata.v1.market_feed_configs.vintage_validity.
It computes every FX feed's vintage availability live, at
read time, through feed_controller::validate -- there is no
stored column and no trigger, so the answer is correct even
straight after an import that added observation rows and no
feed rows. A feed whose price_source is not "vintage" comes
back applicable=false and is not checked.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::simulate_handler {
    +simulate_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>)
    +simulate(msg : nats::message)
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::simulate_handler"
Serves synthetic.v1.fx_spot.simulate. Builds the real GMM
price process and steps it to answer a whole batch of sample
paths at once. Nothing is persisted and nothing is
published: it is a dry run so a caller can see the configured
behaviour before starting a feed.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::ir_curve_preview_handler {
    +ir_curve_preview_handler(nats : nats::client&, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>)
    +simulate_paths(msg : nats::message)
    +preview_shape(msg : nats::message)
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::ir_curve_preview_handler"
The curve-editor dry runs. simulate_paths is the IR
equivalent of simulate_handler and draws short-rate paths.
preview_shape has no FX equivalent, because FX has one scalar
spot where a curve has a tenor grid: it resolves the request's
Curve Template entries through the shared resolver and prices
each one at one process state, so the editor can draw the
shape those entries imply. Both are stateless -- nothing is
persisted or published -- and both clamp their inputs to the
protocol's own maxima.
end note

class ores::synthetic::service::registrar {
    {static} +register_handlers(nats : nats::client&, auth_nats : nats_client&, ctrl : shared_ptr<feed_controller>, ctx : database::context, verifier : optional<jwt_authenticator>) : vector<subscription>
}
note right of "ores::synthetic::service::registrar"
The control-plane registrar, distinct from
ores.synthetic.core's entity registrar. It subscribes nine
subjects, all on the ores.synthetic.service queue:
synthetic.v1.feed_configs.start/.stop/.list,
synthetic.v1.fx_spot.simulate,
synthetic.v1.ir_curve.simulate_paths,
synthetic.v1.ir_curve.preview_shape,
marketdata.v1.market_feed_configs.start_folder/.stop_folder
and marketdata.v1.market_feed_configs.vintage_validity. Each
lambda constructs its handler per message.
end note

